# GETPWENT(3)

## NAME

getpwent, getpwuid, getpwnam, setpwent, endpwent - get password file entry

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <pwd.h>

struct passwd *getpwent(void);

struct passwd *getpwuid(int uid);

struct passwd *getpwnam(const char *name);

void setpwent(void);

void endpwent(void);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Getpwent, getpwuid* and *getpwnam* each return a pointer to an object with the following
structure containing the broken-out fields of a line in the password file.

> ```c
> struct passwd {
>     char *pw_name;
>     char *pw_passwd;
>     int   pw_uid;
>     int   pw_gid;
>     int   pw_quota;
>     char *pw_comment;
>     char *pw_gecos;
>     char *pw_dir;
>     char *pw_shell;
> };
> ```

**Note:** v7's page pulled this in with a **.so** of the installed header. It is written out
here, because there is no installed /usr/include on this system - the one header tree is
share/besm6/include.

The fields *pw_quota* and *pw_comment* are unused; the others have meanings described in
passwd(5). *Getpwent* sets them to 0 and "" respectively, as v7's did.

*Getpwent* reads the next line (opening the file if necessary); *setpwent* rewinds the file;
*endpwent* closes it.

*Getpwuid* and *getpwnam* search from the beginning until a matching *uid* or *name* is found
(or until EOF is encountered).

**Note:** <pwd.h> declares all five now. v7's header declared the struct alone and left the
routines to K&R's implicit **int**; there is no implicit anything here. This header and <grp.h>
are the deliberate exceptions to the tree's rule that a routine no v7 header declares should
declare itself at its own file head - they are not headers added to declare a function, they
are the headers these functions were always part of.

## FILES

| /etc/passwd
| share/besm6/lib/libc.a, lib/libc/stdio/getpwent.c, getpwnam.c, getpwuid.c

## SEE ALSO

getlogin(3), getgrent(3), passwd(5), getpw(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Null pointer (0) returned on EOF or error.

## BUGS

All information is contained in a static area so it must be copied if it is to be saved.
**Every pointer in the struct aims into one shared line buffer,** and so does the struct
itself; the next call overwrites both.

## BESM-6 NOTES

The line buffer is **PWLINE** characters, not BUFSIZ.

v7 wrote **char line[BUFSIZ+1]**, where its BUFSIZ was 512 bytes. **BUFSIZ is 3072 here** - one
disk block, in bytes - so spelling it that way would put 513 words of bss in this file and
another 513 next door in getgrent(3). It looks like tidying and is not.

One v7 bug is fixed rather than carried. A line with fewer than three colons walked off the end
of the caller's buffer: v7 guarded two of its field walks against a `\n` that cannot be there,
the line having already been stripped. The walks are bounded now.

lib/test/pwent is the test, and it prints *no line of /etc/passwd at all:* it checks instead
that every entry the walk yields is found again by name and by id, because an expectation file
may record only what the program does and nothing host-dependent.
